   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to repeal paragraph d of subdivision 2 of section 2-d of the education law, relating to certain provisions relating to the chief privacy officer",
    "basePrintNo": "S3934",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3934-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Rpld §2-d sub 2 ¶ d, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3934\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to repeal paragraph d of subdivision 2 of section 2-d of the\neducation law, relating to certain provisions relating to the chief\nprivacy officer\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo repeal the requirement that a Chief Privacy Officer may hold more\nthan one position at the State Education Department.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Section 1 repeals paragraph d of subdivision 2 of section\n2-d of the education law.\n\nSection 2: Section 2 sets forth an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nCurrent law allows for a Chief Privacy Officer to hold more than one\nposition within the State Education Department.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe 2014-2015 Enacted Budget included comprehensive student data and\nprivacy legislation to ensure an appropriate balance between necessary\ndata being used in a responsible way by educators and schools and the\nprotection of such elements of that data that relate to the personal\nidentifiers of students and teachers. One of the most critical aspects\nof the legislation was to establish a Chief Privacy Officer (CPO)\nwithin the State Education Department (SED). The CPO would be in\ncharge of ensuring that appropriate balance is maintained throughout\nthe State by establishing a Parents Bill of Rights for Data Privacy\nand Security and being a contact person for the public to get\ninformation and report incidents of possible malfeasance or a data\nbreach, among other responsibilities.\n\nThe legislation as enacted was intended to allow SED flexibility in\nhiring a CPO from within the department, if that is what SED chose to\ndo, by stating that the CPO \"may\" hold more than one position within\nthe department. It was not the intent of the legislation to state\nconclusively that the CPO \"must\" be hired from within SED and that he\nor she \"must\" hold more than one position within the department.\nUnfortunately, it has been interpreted by the Division of Budget (DOB)\nthat the language as enacted created a \"part-time\" position at SED,\nwhen in fact it should be a full time position. The enacted\nlegislation set forth stringent hiring criteria that required the CPO\nto be, among other things, intimately knowledgeable about information\nsystems and the educational system in the State. All told the\nrequirements of being the CPO and the responsibilities that the CPO is\nto undertake are that of a full time position within SED.\n\nSED has yet to fill the position of CPO, primarily because it has been\ndetermined by DOB to be a part time position, when in fact it is not.\n\n\nBy repealing this paragraph, it will clarify that the position is\nindeed a full time position and will significantly help SED hire a\nCPO. To ensure our students' and teachers' personal data is protected,\nit is imperative that a CPO be hired as expeditiously as possible.\nThis legislation will ensure that happens.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2015: New bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNo new fiscal implication. This legislation would help the State\nEducation Department hire an appropriately qualified Chief Privacy\nOfficer.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
